+kernel on ``dequantize_rows`` (W3: that check failed; the row-subset and permutation checks passed). The dequantized
+tile now passes an impure identity (``_opaque``: one ``mov.b32`` per two values) that the upcast analysis does not look
+through, so both caches feed the dots in the same layout and the FP8 kernel is the bf16 kernel after the tile
+(``tests/b12x_onepass_ptx.py`` checks the layouts offline). The chunked kernels (decode, verify, exact and FP8
+production prefill today) are untouched.
+
+Row independence (0085): the grid is (R, ceil(H / BMQ)) with BMQ fixed by H (the weights); a program reads only its
+row's query, count and token list and walks the list in order in KT-token tiles, one fixed chain of mma and online-
+softmax steps whatever R, the other rows, the chunk or the lean / pipelined schedule. Nothing is split by R.
+
+Why it need not equal the chunked kernel's bits, and where it may run. It is not bit-identical to
+``latent.sparse_latent`` (the chunk merge's rescaling is gone). A kernel with other bits may serve only rows that
+are never recomputed by another kernel in the same configuration. Fast chunks are exactly such rows under patches/
+0080 / 0085: a fast-prefilled row is reused only through a snapshot of its own tag (fast G + 4 x the b12x bits) at a
+multiple of 64, a resumed request re-prefills every later row with fast chunks (so with this kernel), a reply's rows
+(decoded by the chunked kernel) are never kept in a fast snapshot, and exact requests never resume fast snapshots.
+Hence with bit 4 on: resumed == fresh (both prefill every row past the snapshot with fast chunks), drafted == serial
+(the same deterministic prefill, then the unchanged decode / verify kernels), batched == alone (batch pieces run
+``decode.prefill`` per slot; decode rounds never set ``b.fast``). Using it for decode / verify too is therefore not
+needed; it would also be slow there (one program a row and head tile: 1-2 programs for a 1-row step).
